# Production resource overrides that differ from docker-compose.yml's shared defaults.
#
# Unlike staging.sample.env, this is not a complete override of every resource variable -
# production's containers currently set none of them at all (P114: they predate the cpu_shares
# keys entirely, so they sit at the kernel's default weight of 1024). Fixing that is a separate,
# larger change - recreating every production container from the current compose file - tracked
# by P114 itself. This file holds what P125 and X28 asked for: the app tier's CPU, and the
# database sizing that stopped being optional once the app tier stopped being the only wall.
#
# capacity-content (2026-09-15) found the app container pegged at the shared CPU_LIMIT__APP
# default of 2 cores under 500+ concurrent users - 84.41% throttled at 1,000 - after the query-
# level fixes had already brought Postgres CPU down to 0.50-0.63 mean cores. The bottleneck is
# the app container's own CPU allocation, uniformly across every endpoint, not any one query.
# Production has cores the shared host's other stacks don't, so only production's app tier gets
# more - raising the shared default instead would hand staging and every local checkout the same
# increase and let them compete with production for it, which is exactly what P114 exists to stop.
#
# Copy into production's .env on damballa (or add to it) and recreate urbanlens_production_app and
# urbanlens_production_db - a running container's --cpus and command are both fixed at creation,
# per Docker, so editing .env alone does nothing until the container is recreated. damballa has 16
# cores and 125GB, shared with the staging stack; these are ceilings rather than reservations, so
# app 8 plus db 6 leaves staging to compete on cpu_shares rather than being locked out.
#
# test_production_app_cpu_allocation.py fails if this drops to or below the shared default, or if
# another environment's app-tier limit rises to meet it.
#
# Since measured with tests/perf/k6/population.js on a 4-core/2-core app/db split (2026-09-19),
# drawing tiles, at 30s think time. Two cores throttled the app 17% at 200 users and 78% at
# 1,000, for a ceiling near 175; four cores throttle under 1%. Cores alone were not enough -
# at 250 users every one of the 12 request threads was busy and page p95 sat at 1,034ms, so the
# worker count matters as much as the allocation. Raising it to 6 (24 threads) took page p95 to
# 391ms at 250 users and 395ms at 350, with tile p95 at 27-32ms.
#
# Past that the database was the wall, not the app: its own 2-core limit throttled 23.79% at 500
# users and 84.78% at 750. The 750 and 1,000 levels also saturated the measuring host, so treat
# them as a direction rather than a number, and 500 as marginal.
#
# X28 (2026-09-21) re-measured the whole ladder with the database on four cores and the tuning
# below. 500 concurrent users pass every latency budget but one, the app tier at 2.01 mean cores
# of 4 and Postgres at 1.13 of 4. 1,000 users fail, and on the app tier's CPU alone: it wanted
# 4.02 cores, had 4, and throttled 32.89% of its periods, taking p95 from 231ms to 6.2s across one
# doubling. Nothing errored at any level - over-capacity here is latency.
#
# The values below are sized for 1,000. **Only the 500-user half is measured.** App CPU is linear
# in users at 0.40 cores per 100 across 100-500, so 1,000 users want about 8 cores to run at the
# same 50% utilisation 500 users ran at; the memory figure assumes per-worker cost stays flat,
# which it did from 100 users to 1,000. Re-measure after deploying rather than trusting this.

CPU_LIMIT__APP=8

# 12 workers x gunicorn's 4 gthread threads. The default of 3 leaves 12 threads, which is fewer
# than one map load's tiles, so a viewport queues behind itself before any of it reaches the DB.
# 48 threads is 48 held connections against ul_web's CONNECTION LIMIT of 54, which is the real
# ceiling on this number - 13 workers is the most the role budget accepts, and the whole budget
# sums to the 97 a stock Postgres leaves non-superusers. test_connection_budget_wiring.py checks it.
WEB_CONCURRENCY=12

# Measured on both worker counts at 500 users: 6 workers peak at 1,731MiB and 12 at 3,180MiB.
# A worker recycling on --max-requests briefly runs alongside its replacement, so 12 workers want
# ~3,421MiB - 84% of this limit. It holds, and it is not roomy: 16 workers would not fit under 4g.
# The footprint is per worker rather than per user: it moved 12MiB from 100 users to 1,000.
MEM_LIMIT__APP=4g

# Postgres. Production's db container predates all of this: it runs bare `postgres` with no
# arguments and no cgroup limits at all, so it is on the stock 128MB shared_buffers and the stock
# 4.0 random_page_cost. Both need a recreate to pick up docker-compose.yml's command, not just a
# restart.
#
# Measured at 0.226 database cores per 100 users (0.26 at 100, 0.55 at 250, 1.13 at 500), so 1,000
# users actually served want ~2.3 mean cores. The mean is not what sizes this: peak/mean was 1.9 at
# 500 users, which puts the peak near 4.3. Six is headroom over that, not a second wall being
# cleared. Postgres throttled 0.08% of its periods at 500 users and 0.16% at 1,000.
CPU_LIMIT__DB=6

# 1,403MiB peak at 1,000 users and 1,832MiB after eleven hours up, of which 1GiB is shared_buffers
# itself. It grows with uptime rather than with load, so size it against the larger figure: 4g
# leaves room for that plus the page cache the planner is told below to expect.
MEM_LIMIT__DB=4g

# Stock is 128MB, smaller than the database. Sized against MEM_LIMIT__DB, not the 125GB host: the
# cgroup is what bounds the container's page cache, so effective_cache_size follows the limit.
UL_DB_SHARED_BUFFERS=1GB
UL_DB_EFFECTIVE_CACHE_SIZE=3GB
